<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Basavakalyan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kalyani (Karnataka)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Basavakalyan, historically Kalyani, is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a> district, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. It was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire</a> from the mid-eleventh century and later of the Kalachuris of Kalyani, and in the twelfth century it was the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingayatism">Lingayat</a> movement led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basava">Basava</a>, after whom it was renamed. Its fort and ruins survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/basavakalyan.jpg" alt="Basavakalyan" width="320" height="362" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sscheral · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basava_Gaint_Statue_108_feet,_Basava_Kalyana.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bijapur compensated by expanding south, but after Muhammad's death in 1656 the succession of the young Ali Adil Shah II prompted a Mughal invasion under Prince Aurangzeb, who took Bidar and <b>Kalyani</b> in 1657.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bijapur-sultanate">Bijapur Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Its name outlived it: the Eastern Chalukyas ruled Vengi until the eleventh century, and the Western Chalukyas of <b>Kalyani</b>, who rose in 973, claimed descent from the house of Vatapi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Western Chalukya Empire, also called the empire of the Chalukyas of <b>Kalyani</b> or the Later Chalukyas, was founded in 973 by Tailapa II, a Rashtrakuta feudatory who governed lands around Tardavadi in the Bijapur region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>As Chalukya power weakened, their chief Bijjala II, a commander and minister of the Chalukya king Taila III, took control of the capital <b>Kalyani</b> around 1156 and by 1162 proclaimed himself emperor.</blockquote><a href="/state/kalachuris-of-kalyani">Kalachuris of Kalyani</a></li></ul></section>
